```suggestion
	/* Has to be a multiple of 64 bytes */
	uint64_t size = ROUND_UP(state->total_data_size, SHA256_PADDING_DATA_BYTES);
```

I also have a question: the comment says that `size` must be multiple of 64 bytes, but `SHA256_PADDING_DATA_BYTES` is 9. Shouldn't we use `SHA256_BLOCK_SIZE_BYTES` instead here?

Here we always call `add_zero_bytes` passing the difference between 2 `size_t` values. When `SHA256_PADDING_DATA_BYTES > padding_size_bytes` this results in a very large `size_t` which is then cast to `int32_t` inside `add_zero_bytes`. This looks a bit tricky to me; I think that adding something like:

```
if (padding_size_bytes > SHA256_PADDING_DATA_BYTES) {
    add_zero_bytes(state, padding_size_bytes - SHA256_PADDING_DATA_BYTES);
}
```

would simply the read and also the implementation inside `add_zero_bytes` since you don't need the cast to `int32_t` there. Wdyt?

