Nitpicking: fit is single lines:
```c
typedef int (*counter_api_capture_enable)(const struct device *dev, uint8_t chan);
typedef int (*counter_api_capture_disable)(const struct device *dev, uint8_t chan);

The prescaler configuration switch statement has a default case that returns -EINVAL, but there's no handling for when no prescaler flags are provided (i.e., when (flags & COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV_MSK) equals 0). If a user doesn't explicitly set prescaler flags, this will match case 0 which is COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV1, so DIV1 becomes the implicit default. While this is probably the desired behavior, it should be documented that DIV1 is the default if no prescaler flag is specified, or the code should explicitly handle the zero case for clarity.
```suggestion
	{
		uint32_t prescaler_flags = flags & COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV_MSK;

		if (prescaler_flags == 0U) {
			/* No prescaler flag specified: use DIV1 as the default */
			config_flags |= LL_TIM_ICPSC_DIV1;
		} else {
			switch (prescaler_flags) {
			case COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV1:
				config_flags |= LL_TIM_ICPSC_DIV1;
				break;
			case COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV2:
				config_flags |= LL_TIM_ICPSC_DIV2;
				break;
			case COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV4:
				config_flags |= LL_TIM_ICPSC_DIV4;
				break;
			case COUNTER_CAPTURE_STM32_PRESCALER_DIV8:
				config_flags |= LL_TIM_ICPSC_DIV8;
				break;
			default:
				return -EINVAL;
			}
		}
```

The STM32 driver allows the same channel to be configured for both alarm and capture modes, but there's no check to prevent conflicts. When counter_stm32_capture_callback_set is called, it doesn't verify whether the channel is already in use by an alarm (chdata->callback). Similarly, counter_stm32_set_alarm at line 311 only checks if chdata->callback is set, but doesn't check if the channel is configured for capture mode (chdata->capture). This could lead to undefined behavior if both are configured on the same channel. Either the driver should prevent this by checking both callbacks, or the interrupt handler logic at line 545 that discriminates between modes should be documented as the intended mutual exclusion mechanism.
```suggestion

	/* Prevent configuring capture on a channel already used for alarms */
	if (chdata->callback != NULL) {
		LOG_ERR("%s: Channel %d already configured for alarm, cannot set capture", dev->name, chan);
		return -EBUSY;
	}
```

Pinctrl error handling is overly strict and could prevent driver initialization even when capture functionality is not used. The pinctrl_apply_state call at line 441 returns an error if CONFIG_COUNTER_CAPTURE is enabled but no pinctrl configuration is provided in device tree. However, this breaks backward compatibility - existing device trees without pinctrl configuration will fail to initialize even if they don't use capture. Consider making this conditional: only fail if capture functions are actually called, or check if pcfg is non-NULL before applying. Alternatively, document that when CONFIG_COUNTER_CAPTURE is enabled, pinctrl must be configured in the device tree.
```suggestion
	if (cfg->pcfg) {
		r = pinctrl_apply_state(cfg->pcfg, PINCTRL_STATE_DEFAULT);
		if (r < 0) {
			LOG_ERR("%s: Counter Capture pinctrl setup failed (%d)", dev->name, r);
			return r;
		}
```

The function sets chdata->capture and chdata->user_data at lines 713-714, then enables the interrupt at line 720, creating a potential race condition. If an interrupt fires between setting the callback and completing the configuration (line 717), the callback could be invoked with incomplete hardware configuration. Consider enabling the interrupt only after all configuration is complete, or disable interrupts during this critical section.
```suggestion
	/* Apply configuration */
	LL_TIM_IC_Config(timer, LL_TIM_CHANNEL_CH1 << (4 * chan), config_flags);

	chdata->capture = cb;
	chdata->user_data = user_data;
```

The capture_disable function should clear the capture_cb to prevent potential issues. Unlike alarms which are one-shot and automatically clear their callbacks, capture callbacks are persistent and continue to fire on each edge. When a capture channel is disabled, the callback should be cleared to avoid unexpected behavior if the channel is re-enabled without explicitly setting a new callback. Consider adding `chdata->capture_cb = NULL;` after disabling the channel, similar to how counter_stm32_cancel_alarm clears the alarm_cb at line 327.
```suggestion
	TIM_TypeDef *timer = config->timer;
	struct counter_stm32_ch_data *chdata = &config->ch_data[chan];

	LL_TIM_CC_DisableChannel(timer, LL_TIM_CHANNEL_CH1 << (4 * chan));
	chdata->capture_cb = NULL;
```

Missing interrupt disable: When disabling capture on a channel, the interrupt should be disabled by calling disable_it[chan](timer), similar to how alarm cancellation works (line 342). Without this, interrupts may continue to fire even after the channel is disabled.
```suggestion
	LL_TIM_CC_DisableChannel(timer, ch2ll[chan - 1]);
	disable_it[chan](timer);
```

Thanks @XenuIsWatching - I hope this can be merged soon :rocket: 
I have tested your driver with the following code, which could perhaps be added as a sample
<details> <summary>main.c  (expand)</summary>

```
#include <zephyr/kernel.h>
#include <zephyr/drivers/counter.h>

#define CAPTURE_CHANNEL_ID 0
#define CAPTURE_FLAGS (COUNTER_CAPTURE_CONTINUOUS | COUNTER_CAPTURE_FALLING_EDGE)

#define SAMPLE_TIMER DT_INST(0, st_stm32_counter)


static void counter_capture_cb(const struct device *counter_dev, uint8_t chan_id,
				     counter_capture_flags_t flags, uint32_t cap_ticks,
				     void *user_data)
{
	uint32_t now_ticks;
	uint64_t now_nsec, cap_nsec;

	counter_get_value(counter_dev, &now_ticks);

	now_nsec = counter_ticks_to_ns(counter_dev, now_ticks);
	cap_nsec = counter_ticks_to_ns(counter_dev, cap_ticks);

	printk("Interrupt latency %lld ns \n", now_nsec-cap_nsec);
}

int main(void)
{
	const struct device *const counter_dev = DEVICE_DT_GET(SAMPLE_TIMER);
	int err;

	printk("Counter capture sample\n\n");

	if (!device_is_ready(counter_dev)) {
		printk("device not ready.\n");
		return 0;
	}

	counter_start(counter_dev);

	err = counter_capture_configure(counter_dev, CAPTURE_CHANNEL_ID, CAPTURE_FLAGS, &counter_capture_cb, NULL);
	err |= counter_enable_capture(counter_dev, CAPTURE_CHANNEL_ID);

	if (err != 0) {
		printk("Error %d\n", err);
	}
	else {
		printk("Counter capture armed\n");
	}

	while (1) {
		k_sleep(K_FOREVER);
	}
	return 0;
}
```

</details>

