Setting `err = -ENOEXEC` without returning means execution continues to line 372 which returns `err`. However, when an error occurs at line 361, the mutex should be unlocked before returning. The current code doesn't unlock the mutex on this error path.
```suggestion
		bt_cap_common_unlock_proc(active_proc);

		return -ENOEXEC;
```

bt_cap_common_clear_proc() unlocks `active_proc_mutex` before clearing the struct. That reintroduces a race where another thread can read/modify `active_proc` while it is being memset, and it also mixes non-atomic writes with atomic reads of `proc_state_flags`. Clear the procedure while still holding the mutex (and preferably clear `proc_state_flags` via atomic operations), then unlock afterwards.
```suggestion
	/* Clear state flags atomically before clearing the rest of the struct */
	(void)atomic_clear(&proc->proc_state_flags);

	(void)memset(proc, 0, sizeof(*proc));

	bt_cap_common_unlock_proc(proc);
```

This code calls bt_cap_initiator_qos_configured() while still holding active_proc_mutex. bt_cap_initiator_qos_configured() also locks the same mutex and may clear the procedure, which can violate bt_cap_common_clear_proc()'s lock_count==1 assertion. Unlock before invoking the next-stage handler, or refactor to a non-locking internal helper.
```suggestion
		bt_cap_common_unlock_proc();
		bt_cap_initiator_qos_configured(active_proc->proc_param.initiator[0].stream);
```

