I think this entire `else` needs to be guarded by `CONFIG_BT_BAP_UNICAST_CLIENT_ASE_SRC`. The `BT_CAP_COMMON_SUBPROC_TYPE_STOP` part of the procedure is only valid when we are stopping SRC ASEs. 

So if we do something like 

```c
	else if (IS_ENABLED(CONFIG_BT_BAP_UNICAST_CLIENT_ASE_SRC)) {
		    ...
		    bt_cap_common_set_subproc(BT_CAP_COMMON_SUBPROC_TYPE_STOP);
		    ...
	} else {
		struct bt_cap_initiator_proc_param *proc_param;

		if (!bt_cap_common_proc_is_done()) {
			/* We are still disabling or stopping some */
			bt_cap_common_unlock_proc();

			return;
		}

		bt_cap_common_set_subproc(BT_CAP_COMMON_SUBPROC_TYPE_RELEASE);

		proc_param = get_next_proc_param(active_proc);
		if (proc_param == NULL) {
			/* If proc_param is NULL then this step is a no-op and we can finish the
			 * procedure
			 */
			cap_initiator_unicast_audio_proc_complete(active_proc);

			return;
		} /* wait for bt_cap_initiator_qos_configured */
	}
```

I _think_ it should work; this will effectively make the CAP Initiator skip the `BT_CAP_COMMON_SUBPROC_TYPE_STOP` subprocedure completely.

In `bt_cap_initiator_disabled()`, the new `else` branch for non-`CONFIG_BT_BAP_UNICAST_CLIENT_ASE_SRC` sets the sub-procedure to RELEASE and may `return` (or just exit the function) without unlocking `active_proc_mutex` when `proc_param != NULL`. This can leave the CAP procedure mutex locked and deadlock subsequent callbacks/operations. Ensure the function calls `bt_cap_common_unlock_proc()` (or `bt_cap_common_clear_proc()` when completing) on all exit paths, including when `proc_param` is non-NULL and you intend to wait for the next callback.
```suggestion
		}

		/* Wait for bt_cap_initiator_qos_configured */
		bt_cap_common_unlock_proc();
```

