> So I want to be very clear on this before I implement this feature for the third time, because the consequences of your suggestions just lead back to the reasons the first attempt in #98541 was rejected.

I think breaking the API in this case will result in a more efficient and scalable solution, at quite little cost to users of pipes, and implementers of backends:

Given we are modifying the API to bring efficiency enhancements, not functional ones, we can add thin wrappers on both the user and backend sides of the pipe transmit APIs:
```
static int pipe_call_transmit_chain(struct modem_pipe *pipe,
                                    const struct modem_pipe_fragment *fragments,                            
                                    size_t fragments_size)
{
        int ret;
        int transmitted;

        if (pipe->api->transmit_chain == NULL) {
                for (size_t i = 0; i < fragments_size; i++) {
                        ret = pipe->api->transmit(pipe->data, fragments[i].data, fragments[i].size);
                        if (ret <= 0) {
                                return ret;
                        }

                        transmitted += ret;
                };

                return transmitted;
        }

	return pipe->api->transmit_chain(pipe->data, fragments, fragments_size);
}
```
which would compliment the thin wrapper for `modem_pipe_transmit` below. We could then deprecate the transmit API over a release.

> > I don't want us to have to break the API again in the future to add a third buffer
> 
> Point 1, this PR is the exact opposite of a breaking change, neither backends nor users of the API need to do anything to continue operating correctly.
>
> > and I don't want to carry around two transmit APIs
> > ...
> > If we add transmit_double or transmit_chain, transmit should be a thin wrapper on top of either of those.
> 
> [#98541 (review)](https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/pull/98541#pullrequestreview-3397782650) rejected the proposal because
> 
> > This API change spans through way too many modules and causes API breaks for pipe backends that are implemented outside of Zephyr tree
> 
> Point 2, presumably the "thin wrapper" you suggest would look something like:
> 
> ```
> static inline int pipe_transmit(struct modem_pipe *pipe, const uint8_t *buf, size_t size)
> {
>     struct modem_pipe_fragment frag = {
>         .data = buf, .size = size,
>     };
> 
>     return pipe->api->transmit_chain(pipe, &frag, 1);
> }
> ```
> 
> Combined with your comment about not wanting two transmit API's, presumably the `transmit` backend function disappears, leading to exactly the breaking changes and OOT problems that led to @SeppoTakalo blocking the first PR.
>
> `pipe_transmit_chain` is not much different from this PR from an implementation perspective, but it has a number of consequences that this PR does not that I want approved beforehand before spending yet more time on this.

With the proposed wrapper for the transmit API on the backend side, this would not be the case I believe

> I'm not a fan of the `flush/configure` pattern. You say `"Its not a breaking API change"` but it sort of is, every backend would need to be updated to conform with the API, its not described as a "may or may not do anything" option. Which IMO is actually worse, as it leads to divergence from the API behavior without any corresponding compiler errors.

I don't think we should go with this either because of the added complexity of adding "statefullness" to the backends where they need to "save" half wrapped packets between calls to transmit if BUFFERED option is set.

In summary, I prefer the chained API, and I believe we can implement it without any breaking changes, and can decide to deprecate the transmit API later to get back to a single API implemented by the backends. The `modem_pipe_transmit ` thin wrapper seems like it would remain useful.

