Sorry about a bit too long code example.
The logic that I'm trying to show here is:
* When GNSS is needed, it requests a modem with `pm_device_runtime_get()`
* When pipelink is opened, we know that the modem is active, CMUX is open and we can open the DLC 3.
* When all pipes are opened, we need to send AT command to modem in `init_gnss_work_handler()`. This is a missing API from the modem driver currently, but I don't see why it would not be supported.

The logic does not depend on the state of the PPP interfaces.
Modem is powered or unpowered depending on PM runtime state.

> What we don't have ye is bringing the interface down parks the modem at AWAIT_DIAL but never touches CFUN and the radio stays fully functional and registered the whole time, so on shared-antenna hardware GNSS still wouldn't get a clear RF path. The missing piece isn't GNSS-aware arbitration in the driver, it's exposing CFUN/low-power control to the application at all. Right now it's hardcoded into the init/dial scripts with no application hook. That would be a small, generic addition, not GNSS-specific, and the app would still own the actual policy, which is where I think it belongs.

I think the key 'problem' to solve here is splitting out the modem base functionality (booting, setting up CMUX) from the higher level functionalities (PPP/GNSS). Right now `modem_cellular` is making the assuming that a PM request is a request for PPP connectivity. This is driven from the `modem_ppp` file:
https://github.com/zephyrproject-rtos/zephyr/blob/52b48529aecc3ad0ef9b159996bdb5d2b3e5b794/subsys/modem/modem_ppp.c#L552-L561

Any solution which can handle GNSS as a mutually exclusive operation will need to break this assumption. `pm_device_runtime_get` should be a necessary pre-condition for the PPP interface, but it shouldn't be the only API call required. Something along the lines of:
```
static bool dial_requested;

/* Called once CMUX setup */
static void on_modem_cmux_active()
{
   modem_cellular_dial();
}

static int modem_ppp_ppp_api_start(const struct device *dev)
{
    dial_requested= true;
    pm_device_runtime_get(modem);
}

static int modem_ppp_ppp_api_stop(const struct device *dev)
{
    modem_cellular_hangup();
    pm_device_runtime_put(modem);
}
```

@Thalley

> nor do I agree with the overall approach that BT uses to do this.

I agree that using cooperative scheduling as implicit locking is something to remove, not turn into a broader application contract. My support here is for relaxing an unnecessary restriction in Host-less builds while preserving existing behavior elsewhere. The selects don't solve the underlying problem: keeping Mesh's system workqueue cooperative would not preserve its synchronization assumptions if Host callbacks became preemptible. That still requires the coordinated client audit and adaptations discussed above as part of #116316.

@Thalley @waihongtam

Withdrawing the prompted option I asked for, `BT_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_COOP_REQUIRED` variant included. A `default` is not enforcement: an earlier definition of the option, in a board `Kconfig.defconfig` or an application `Kconfig`, takes precedence over a later `default y`. Give it `default n` there and a build with `CONFIG_BT=y`, `BT_HCI_HOST=y` and the helper selected ends up with the option off, accepting `CONFIG_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_PRIORITY=10` without a warning. The same configuration on the current head fails at Kconfig as it should, because a `select` is a lower bound on the value while a `default` is not.

That settles the `select` question too. tips.rst warns against selecting symbols that have prompts or dependencies, and `BT_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_COOP` as it stands has neither, which is the case the same page says to select as much as you like. The prompt I asked for is what would have turned it into the discouraged kind.

It also bought nothing. On the current head an HCI-raw build with no requirer takes priority 10 with zero warnings and the option simply unset, which is the configuration this PR is for, and an explicit `CONFIG_BT_SYSTEM_WORKQUEUE_COOP=n` on a Host build already errors out naming the option.

